On Monday 19 April 2010 10:24:20 Ian Johnson wrote:
> Sorry to interject, but just so I understand:
>
> Define a rafsi R1 to be M1+C1, with M1 being an established cmavo such
> as {na} and C1 being a consonant, and define another rafsi R2 whose first
> consonant is C2. If C1+R2 is morphologically acceptable, then to form lujvo
> R1+R2 we need a y in between C1 and C2, even if C1+C2 is an acceptable
> consonant cluster, to avoid fragmentation into M1 C1+R2?
>
> Please pardon my horrible abuse of the addition operator, heh.

Correct. Another example is "cakyrespa"; since "krespa" is a lujvo, "y" is 
needed in "cakyrespa". "cakremna" does not need "y" because "kremna" is not 
valid. "baklanme" is valid but "bakladru" breaks up, so if you mean cow milk, 
it's "bakyladru". This rule is called "tosmabru"; where that name came from 
I'm not sure, since "tos" isn't the rafsi of anything.

